# Compliance, Quality & Documentation Standards

This rule ensures all development follows best practices for dependency management, security compliance (ISO 27001, DSGVO/GDPR), and documentation.

## 1. NPM Module Management

### Always Use Current Versions

Before any `git push`, ALWAYS:

```bash
# 1. Check for outdated packages
ncu -u

# 2. Install updated packages
npm install

# 3. Run full build to verify compatibility
npm run build

# 4. Run all tests
npm test

# 5. Only then commit and push
git add .
git commit -m "chore: update dependencies"
git push
```

### Dependency Update Checklist

- [ ] Run `ncu -u` to check for updates
- [ ] Review breaking changes in CHANGELOG of major updates
- [ ] Update `package.json` with new versions
- [ ] Run `npm install` to update `package-lock.json`
- [ ] Execute `npm run build` - must complete without errors
- [ ] Execute `npm test` - all tests must pass
- [ ] Test critical functionality manually if major updates

### Security Audits

```bash
# Run security audit before every push
npm audit

# Fix vulnerabilities automatically where possible
npm audit fix

# For critical vulnerabilities
npm audit fix --force
```

### Version Pinning Strategy

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    // Use exact versions for critical dependencies
    "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk": "1.12.1",
    
    // Use caret for trusted packages with good semver
    "zod": "^3.24.0"
  }
}
```

## 2. ISO 27001 Compliance

### Information Security Management

#### A.8 Asset Management

```typescript
// MUST: Classify and protect information assets
interface DataClassification {
  level: 'public' | 'internal' | 'confidential' | 'restricted';
  owner: string;
  retentionPeriod: number; // days
}

// MUST: Tag sensitive data appropriately
const sensitiveFields = ['email', 'password', 'token', 'apiKey', 'secret'];
```

#### A.9 Access Control

```typescript
// MUST: Implement principle of least privilege
interface AccessControl {
  // Users get minimum required permissions
  scopes: string[];
  // Time-limited access tokens
  tokenExpiry: number;
  // Role-based access
  role: 'user' | 'admin' | 'readonly';
}

// MUST: Validate all access attempts
async function validateAccess(token: string, requiredScope: string): Promise<boolean> {
  // Verify token validity
  // Check scope permissions
  // Log access attempt
  return hasAccess;
}
```

#### A.10 Cryptography

```typescript
// MUST: Use strong encryption
const CRYPTO_REQUIREMENTS = {
  // Minimum key lengths
  symmetricKeyLength: 256,  // AES-256
  asymmetricKeyLength: 2048, // RSA-2048 minimum
  hashAlgorithm: 'SHA-256',
  
  // Token requirements
  tokenLength: 32, // bytes
  saltRounds: 12,  // bcrypt
};

// NEVER store secrets in plaintext
// ALWAYS use environment variables or secure vaults
```

#### A.12 Operations Security

```typescript
// MUST: Comprehensive logging (without sensitive data)
interface AuditLog {
  timestamp: string;        // ISO 8601 format
  action: string;           // What happened
  userId: string;           // Who did it (anonymized if needed)
  resource: string;         // What was affected
  result: 'success' | 'failure';
  // NEVER log: passwords, tokens, personal data
}

// MUST: Implement change management
// All changes must be:
// - Version controlled (Git)
// - Reviewed (PR reviews)
// - Tested (automated tests)
// - Documented (CHANGELOG, README)
```

#### A.14 System Acquisition & Development

```typescript
// MUST: Secure development practices
const SECURE_DEVELOPMENT = {
  // Input validation on all boundaries
  validateInput: true,
  
  // Output encoding to prevent XSS
  encodeOutput: true,
  
  // Parameterized queries (Prisma handles this)
  preventSqlInjection: true,
  
  // Security testing in CI/CD
  securityTests: true,
};
```

### ISO 27001 Code Review Checklist

- [ ] No hardcoded credentials or secrets
- [ ] All inputs validated with Zod schemas
- [ ] Sensitive data encrypted at rest and in transit
- [ ] Access controls implemented and tested
- [ ] Audit logging for security-relevant events
- [ ] Error messages don't expose system internals
- [ ] Dependencies checked for vulnerabilities

## 3. DSGVO/GDPR Compliance

### Article 5: Data Processing Principles

```typescript
// Principle: Lawfulness, fairness, transparency
interface DataProcessingBasis {
  legalBasis: 
    | 'consent'           // Art. 6(1)(a)
    | 'contract'          // Art. 6(1)(b)
    | 'legal_obligation'  // Art. 6(1)(c)
    | 'vital_interests'   // Art. 6(1)(d)
    | 'public_interest'   // Art. 6(1)(e)
    | 'legitimate_interest'; // Art. 6(1)(f)
  purpose: string;
  documented: boolean;
}

// Principle: Purpose limitation
// ONLY collect data for specified, explicit purposes
// NEVER use data for incompatible purposes

// Principle: Data minimization
// ONLY collect data that is necessary
interface UserData {
  // ✅ Necessary for service
  email: string;
  
  // ❌ NOT necessary - don't collect
  // dateOfBirth: string;
  // phoneNumber: string;
}
```

### Article 17: Right to Erasure ("Right to be Forgotten")

```typescript
// MUST implement data deletion capability
async function deleteUserData(userId: string): Promise<void> {
  // Delete all personal data
  await deletePersonalRecords(userId);
  
  // Anonymize data that must be retained
  await anonymizeAuditLogs(userId);
  
  // Document deletion
  await logDeletion(userId);
  
  // Notify third parties if data was shared
  await notifyDataRecipients(userId);
}
```

### Article 25: Data Protection by Design & Default

```typescript
// Privacy by Design principles
const PRIVACY_BY_DESIGN = {
  // Proactive not reactive
  preventBreaches: true,
  
  // Privacy as default
  defaultPrivacy: true,
  
  // Privacy embedded into design
  builtIn: true,
  
  // Full functionality - no trade-offs
  noTradeOffs: true,
  
  // End-to-end security
  fullLifecycle: true,
  
  // Visibility and transparency
  transparent: true,
  
  // User-centric
  userCentric: true,
};

// Default settings MUST be most privacy-friendly
const DEFAULT_SETTINGS = {
  shareData: false,
  analyticsEnabled: false,
  marketingEmails: false,
};
```

### Article 32: Security of Processing

```typescript
// MUST implement appropriate technical measures
const SECURITY_MEASURES = {
  // Encryption
  encryptionAtRest: true,
  encryptionInTransit: true, // TLS 1.2+
  
  // Pseudonymization where possible
  pseudonymization: true,
  
  // Regular testing
  securityTesting: 'regular',
  
  // Incident response
  incidentResponsePlan: true,
};
```

### Article 33 & 34: Breach Notification

```typescript
// MUST notify within 72 hours of becoming aware
interface DataBreachNotification {
  discoveredAt: string;
  notifyAuthorityBy: string; // +72 hours
  affectedDataSubjects: number;
  categoriesOfData: string[];
  likelyConsequences: string;
  measuresTaken: string[];
  
  // If high risk to individuals, also notify them
  notifyIndividuals: boolean;
}
```

### DSGVO Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Legal basis for data processing documented
- [ ] Only necessary data collected (data minimization)
- [ ] User consent obtained where required
- [ ] Data deletion capability implemented
- [ ] Privacy policy updated for new features
- [ ] Data processing records maintained
- [ ] Third-party processors have DPA in place
- [ ] Cross-border transfers comply with regulations

## 4. README.md Maintenance

### Always Update README When:

1. **New features added** - Document usage and examples
2. **Configuration changes** - Update environment variables
3. **API changes** - Update endpoint documentation
4. **Dependencies changed** - Update requirements
5. **Breaking changes** - Add migration guide

### README Structure Template

```markdown
# Project Name

Brief description of the project.

## Features

- Feature 1
- Feature 2

## Quick Start

\`\`\`bash
npm install
npm run build
npm start
\`\`\`

## Configuration

| Variable | Description | Required |
|----------|-------------|----------|
| `VAR_1` | Description | Yes |

## API Documentation

### Endpoints

...

## Security

Security considerations and compliance info.

## Contributing

How to contribute.

## License

License information.

## Changelog

Link to CHANGELOG.md
```

### README Update Checklist

- [ ] Version number updated (if applicable)
- [ ] New features documented with examples
- [ ] Configuration/environment variables current
- [ ] Installation instructions tested and working
- [ ] API documentation reflects current state
- [ ] Security section updated for compliance changes
- [ ] All links functional
- [ ] No outdated information

## 5. Pre-Push Workflow

### Complete Pre-Push Checklist

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# pre-push-checklist.sh

echo "🔄 Updating dependencies..."
ncu -u
npm install

echo "🔨 Building project..."
npm run build
if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "❌ Build failed!"
  exit 1
fi

echo "🧪 Running tests..."
npm test
if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "❌ Tests failed!"
  exit 1
fi

echo "🔒 Security audit..."
npm audit
if [ $? -ne 0 ]; then
  echo "⚠️ Security vulnerabilities found!"
  read -p "Continue anyway? (y/n) " -n 1 -r
  if [[ ! $REPLY =~ ^[Yy]$ ]]; then
    exit 1
  fi
fi

echo "📝 Checking README..."
# Ensure README was updated if source changed
git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q "src/" && \
git diff --cached --name-only | grep -q "README.md" || \
echo "⚠️ Source files changed but README not updated!"

echo "✅ All checks passed!"
```

### Git Hooks Recommendation

```bash
# Install husky for git hooks
npm install -D husky

# Create pre-push hook
npx husky add .husky/pre-push "npm run build && npm test"
```

## 6. Commit Message Standards

### Format for Compliance-Related Changes

```bash
# Security fixes
git commit -m "security: fix XSS vulnerability in user input handling"

# DSGVO compliance
git commit -m "compliance(gdpr): implement data deletion for Art. 17"

# Dependency updates
git commit -m "chore(deps): update all dependencies to latest versions"

# Documentation
git commit -m "docs: update README with new API endpoints"
```

